.TH USLEEP 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
usleep \- pause for a number of microseconds
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usleep
.I microseconds
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B usleep
waits for the given number of microseconds, then exits.
It reads no input and writes no output.
This
.B usleep
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
The argument is a string of decimal digits whose value is at most
4294967295.
Leading zeros are allowed and do not make the number octal.
Nothing else is a number: no sign, white space, hexadecimal prefix,
decimal point, exponent, or unit suffix.
Arguments after the first are ignored without being examined.
.PP
The wait is the number of microseconds divided by 1000, rounded down to
whole milliseconds.
A value below 1000 does not wait.
The largest value waits 4294967 milliseconds, about 71 minutes and
35 seconds.
Nothing short of ending the process cuts the wait short.
.SH OPTIONS
.B usleep
has no options.
A first argument other than
.B \-\-help
that begins with
.BR \- ,
including
.BR \-\- ,
is an invalid number.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
In a later position it is ignored like any other extra argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B usleep
reads no environment variables.
The startup code shared by all applets reads
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
and may trim a trailing semicolon from
.BR PATH .
None of these affects how
.B usleep
waits.
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The wait ended, or the first argument was
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
There was no argument, or the first argument was invalid.
Nothing is waited for.
.PP
With no argument,
.B usleep
prints a usage message on standard error.
An invalid first argument is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
usleep: invalid number \(aq\fIargument\fR\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
This includes a value above 4294967295, which is reported as an invalid
number, not as out of range.
.SH EXAMPLES
Pause for a quarter of a second:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ usleep 250000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check for a file ten times a second until it appears:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ until test \-e ready.txt; do usleep 100000; done
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B usleep
is not specified by POSIX.
It takes one decimal integer, with no unit suffix, and has no option
other than
.BR \-\-help .
.PP
The wait is a call to the Windows
.B Sleep
function with the millisecond count, so it ends on a tick of the system
timer.
The tick is typically about 15 ms, which makes a short wait last much
longer than requested: a value from 1000 through 10000 usually takes
about 15 ms.
A value below 1000 is a count of zero milliseconds and returns at once.
.PP
The kit's
.BR sh (1)
runs
.BR usleep ,
an applet that busybox marks
.BR NOFORK ,
inside a shell process rather than in a process of its own.
This holds in a pipeline or subshell too, which runs in a copy of the
shell.
There Ctrl+C cannot interrupt the wait and takes effect only after the
wait is over.
Started in its own process, as
.B busybox usleep
or through the launcher
.IR usleep.exe ,
from the shell or from
.BR cmd.exe ,
it ends at once on Ctrl+C, and the process exits with status
.BR 0xC000013A .
For a wait that Ctrl+C must be able to end in the shell, use
.BR sleep (1),
which the shell runs in another process.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR sleep (1)
